<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div class="brand">
        <h1>Ctrl Alt Deliberate</h1>
        <p class="tagline">Five computer scientists, one big question: can we trust the code if the agent sounds confident?</p>
        <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
          <a href="#team">Meet the team</a>
          <a href="#future-of-se">Our AI outlook</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <button class="theme-toggle" id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
        <span class="theme-icon" aria-hidden="true">☾</span>
        <span class="theme-label">Night mode</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section id="team">
      <div class="intro">
        <h2>Meet the team</h2>
        <p>We’re five computer science students drawn to AI, software, and the fine art of finding the bug that was “definitely not in our code.”</p>
        <p>We bring together cybersecurity, systems thinking, and a healthy respect for edge cases. We’re part of the Agentic Software Engineering Lab at Paderborn University, where we explore how people and coding agents can build software together.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Jenith_avatar.png}}" alt="Avatar of Jenith">
          <h3>Jenith</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Jenith studies computer science at Paderborn University and is interested in cybersecurity, SOC operations, vulnerability assessment, Python, and network systems. AI assistants can speed up boilerplate, tests, and documentation, but they may miss system context or introduce subtle flaws. Jenith believes every generated line deserves a human review. A memorable debugging lesson: one misplaced router rule can turn a network script into a digital traffic jam.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI won't replace human minds; it can provide peace of mind—if we know how to use it.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:prachi_avatar.png}}" alt="Avatar of Prachi">
          <h3>Prachi</h3>
          <p class="study">Master’s in Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Prachi earned a Master’s in Computer Science from Paderborn University and enjoys debugging, SOC operations, and travel. AI is helpful for small coding jobs and simple tests, but code can look correct and still be wrong. In security work, that makes careful human review essential. She expects engineers to spend less time typing routine code and more time checking, testing, designing, and finding bugs. And for the record: Prachi is 23, not 15.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI will not take your job. But someone who can check AI code well might.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:pratham_avatar.jpg}}" alt="Avatar of Pratham">
          <h3>Pratham</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Pratham is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Science at Paderborn University after studying computer science at the University of Mumbai. He is into cybersecurity, AI, anime, and building custom home-theater systems. AI can take care of boilerplate and repetitive tasks, but it may gloss over security details. Pratham thinks engineers will increasingly design systems and audit generated logic, bringing hands-on knowledge of networks and hardware to the job. His DIY audio hobby is basically systems engineering—with better sound.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI will write 90% of our code, and we’ll spend 100% of our time patching the security vulnerabilities it introduces.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:vaibhav_avatar.jpg}}" alt="Avatar of Vaibhav">
          <h3>Vaibhav</h3>
          <p class="study">Master’s in Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Vaibhav studies computer science at Paderborn University and is interested in artificial intelligence, anime, and philosophy. He is also a huge fan of the Harry Potter movies. He sees AI as a major force in software development, cutting down the time spent writing code by hand and fixing bugs. Looking five to ten years ahead, Vaibhav imagines AI handling almost the entire journey from a raw idea to working software. The rest of us have follow-up questions; that’s what makes the future interesting.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI makes shipping software faster, but it trades deep critical thinking for security bugs.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:vatsal_avatar.jpeg}}" alt="Avatar of Vatsal">
          <h3>Vatsal</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Vatsal completed a bachelor’s degree in computer engineering at CHARUSAT University and is now pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Science at Paderborn University. He enjoys AI, anime, volleyball, sightseeing, and dramas. He thinks AI makes it easier and faster to turn ideas into software, and expects it to assist developers across the whole process. Vatsal is reserved at first, but open with friends—which is a useful reminder that good systems, like good conversations, take more than a first impression.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI can handle routine tasks with ease; trusting its security and reliability is the real challenge.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se">
      <h2>The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
      <p class="future-intro">We don’t all predict the same destination, but we agree the job is changing. Our shared notes from the human–agent collaboration department:</p>

      <div class="future-grid">
        <article class="future-card">
          <h3>What we agree on</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>AI is already useful for accelerating routine coding, boilerplate, tests, and other repetitive work.</li>
            <li>Generated code can look convincing and still contain logic mistakes or security problems.</li>
            <li>Testing, review, and human judgment matter more—not less—as the amount of generated code grows.</li>
            <li>Engineers will spend more time on design, complex problem-solving, reliability, and security.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="future-card disagreement">
          <h3>Where we disagree</h3>
          <p>How much of the job will AI eventually do on its own? Vaibhav sees a future where AI could turn a raw idea into finished software with little to no human intervention.</p>
          <p>Others expect people to remain essential as architects, security auditors, and the ones who notice that the “working” demo has quietly opened the wrong port. The timeline—and how much autonomy is wise—is still up for debate.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2025 · Team Ctrl Alt Deliberate
  </footer>

  <script>
    (function () {
      var root = document.documentElement;
      var toggle = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");
      var icon = toggle.querySelector(".theme-icon");
      var label = toggle.querySelector(".theme-label");

      function updateButton(theme) {
        var nextTheme = theme === "dark" ? "day" : "night";
        icon.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "☀" : "☾";
        label.textContent = "Switch to " + nextTheme + " mode";
        toggle.setAttribute("aria-label", "Switch to " + nextTheme + " mode");
      }

      updateButton(root.getAttribute("data-theme"));

      toggle.addEventListener("click", function () {
        var nextTheme = root.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        root.setAttribute("data-theme", nextTheme);
        updateButton(nextTheme);
        try {
          localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", nextTheme);
        } catch (error) {
          /* Theme still changes for this visit if storage is unavailable. */
        }
      });
    }());
  </script>
</body>
</html>
